response:
The straight‑line distance from Earth to the Moon is about **238,900 miles** (384,400 km).

If you drove a car that could keep a constant highway speed of **60 miles per hour (mph)**, the time would be:

\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
            = \frac{238,900 \text{ miles}}{60 \text{ mph}}
            \approx 3,980 \text{ hours}
\]

Convert hours to days:

\[
\frac{3,980 \text{ hours}}{24 \text{ hours/day}} \approx 166 \text{ days}
\]

So it would take **about 5½ months** of nonstop driving at 60 mph to reach the Moon. (This is just a simple distance‑over‑speed calculation. In reality, you would need rockets, fuel, and would encounter many obstacles.)
